@* RadioGroup's Overview, written by hand after Button's, Card's and MultiSelect's: an enum's
   options stacked or in a row, options from a list, a required choice that starts empty, and
   states. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<TextBlock>A radio group is a set of options, one of which the user chooses. It suits short lists where every option should be in view.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>For two to five short options, a <Anchor Href="docs/components/segmented-control">SegmentedControl</Anchor> needs less room. For a long list, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/select">Select</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Stacked or in a row</Heading>
<TextBlock>Options stack by default. To put a few short options in a row, set <Code>Orientation.Horizontal</Code>; the row wraps when there isn't room. Bound to an enum, the group shows each value as an option, named by its <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="radio-group-usage" />

<Heading Level="2">Chips</Heading>
<TextBlock><Code>Chips</Code> shows the options as chips in a wrapping row, the chosen one filled with a check. They're still radio buttons, so the group posts, takes the arrow keys and reads to screen readers as before. For a few options joined in one row, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/segmented-control">SegmentedControl</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="radio-group-chips" />

<Heading Level="2">Options from a list</Heading>
<TextBlock>A group's options can come from a list rather than an enum. Set <Code>Items</Code> to the list. To set how each option reads, set <Code>ItemText</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="radio-group-shipping" />
<TextBlock>Each radio posts its value as text, and Blazor converts it back to the property's type. Use values that convert, such as strings, numbers or enums.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>A form post can contain a value that isn't among the options, and it reaches the model. Check it on the server. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Required, with nothing chosen</Heading>
<TextBlock>A group can start with nothing chosen and require a choice. Bind a nullable property, and give it <Code>[Required]</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="radio-group-required-choice" />
<TextBlock>A property that isn't nullable always has a value, so <Code>[Required]</Code> never fails. The option for its starting value is chosen from the start: an enum's <Code>0</Code>, unless the model sets another.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>A form post can contain any number for an enum property. Before using the value, check that it's one of the enum's values.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>To line the group up with buttons and other fields, set <Code>Size</Code>. For a choice the user can't change, set <Code>Disabled</Code>; a disabled group doesn't post.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>There's no <Code>ReadOnly</Code>, because HTML has no read-only radio. In a read-only <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>, the group is disabled, and a hidden field posts its value.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="radio-group-states" />
<TextBlock>Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

